/-!
# Rho's model is sound for the reference structural congruence

The reference rho calculus (`RhoCalculus.StructuralCongruence`, after Meredith and Radestock) works
on the same patterns. Every generating equality of the model of `GeneralRho.Standard.plan` with the
name equation is a structural congruence there: a permutation of a parallel composition
(`par_perm`) or of a set (`set_perm`), a flattening (`par_flatten` around permutations), a removal of
`PZero` (`par_flatten` and `par_nil_left`), the name equation (`quote_drop`), each in every context.
Equality in the model of object terms is therefore structural congruence (`modelEquiv_sc`).

Every firing of the plan is a reference reduction (`primitive_reduces`): COMM at the focus is the
reference COMM after a permutation of the parallel composition, with the reflective substitution
equal to the reference's semantic substitution on well-sorted closed bodies
(`GeneralRho.reflective_reduct_semantic`), and the standard reduction contexts are nested parallel
compositions (`Reduces.par_any`). Hence every step modulo the model is a reference reduction
(`step_reduces`).

The converse fails exactly at the reference laws the model does not declare, the wrapper laws
`{P} ≡ P` and `{} ≡ PZero` (`WrapperStep`): on object terms, structural congruence is the equality
generated by the model's law moves and the wrapper laws (`sc_iff_reference`). The channels `@{}` and
`@0` are structurally congruent but not equal in the model (`emptyWrapper_gap`). The wrapper laws are
those the declared collection algebra already states (`Wrappers.WrapperStep`), and the model's canonical
form with them decides structural congruence (`sc_iff_modelCanon`).
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace Mettapedia.OSLF.MeTTaIL.Complete.RhoReference

open _root_.Mettapedia.OSLF.MeTTaIL.Syntax GeneralExecutionAdmission CanonicalBags QuoteDrop RhoPresented
